{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffdbq87JS16cS3HZ8ciY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, **`pair_normalized_tally_switch_contrast`**, and it passed the self-check (`OK`). All three files are in `iter_4/candidate_1/`.\n\n**Hypothesis.** People judge the two sequences against each other rather than one at a time. They still compare each sequence's heads-minus-tails tally span with their own expected span, and its switching rate with an ideal rate, with a personal left/right lean. The change is how the gap between the two sequences is weighed: on each cue it is divided by how far the pair as a whole sits from the ideal. A gap decides the choice sharply when both sequences are near the ideal, and only weakly when both are far from it. So the same sequence pulls more or less strongly depending on its partner, which is what the brief asked for. One shared strength parameter `w` controls this; at `w = 0` the model is exactly the current best model (`tally_span_switch_rate_ideal_2`).\n\n**Why this mechanism.** The critique's strongest result that survives the FDR correction is that in pairs where both sequences switch a lot, people pick the more-switching one 0.525 of the time, against the best model's predicted 0.409. Those pairs sit far from the ideal switching rate, so this normalisation makes the choice between them less decisive, moving the prediction toward what people do.\n\nSimpler ways of judging the sequences against each other don't work with these squared distance-from-ideal terms. Pulling the reference point toward the pair's average, or exaggerating each sequence's difference from its partner, only rescales the existing sensitivity, so the data couldn't tell them apart from the current model. Dividing by the pair's total deviation does change the shape of the predictions.\n\n**Self-check results:**\n- **Fit:** the smoke ELPD-LOO was −2241.0; the best model scores −2242.7. This is only a one-chain, 100-draw fit, so it suggests the model is competitive but proves nothing.\n- **PSIS-LOO reliability:** the check warned that 13% of trials had high Pareto-k, which makes the LOO estimate unreliable on that short run. The full fit may or may not clear this.\n- **Not checked:** the novelty gate against the other models, and convergence in the full fit.\n- **Speed:** the 200-iteration smoke fit took 169 s. Admission kills any sampling run after 30 minutes, and if the full fit scales roughly with iterations it may get close to that limit.\n\nThe hypothesis echoes the earlier `pair_normalized_alternation_contrast`, which was pruned 59.8 nats behind the best model in experiment 1. This version is a substantive change, not a copy: it normalises both the tally-span and switching cues of the current best model, and a fitted strength lets it fall back to independent judgement."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3484,"cache_read_input_tokens":76600,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3484,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc5f2bbb-6019-4fe1-85f9-f6d4280d480b","uuid":"d1588b2b-dfac-4a49-8c4a-fe282f84e082","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:38:10.607Z","request_id":"req_011CffdbpBK6iVdjsrSVfgdE"}
